      "kind": "fracture",
      "description": "Whether a string can change IN PLACE. Immutable-value camp: python java csharp go kotlin dart typescript php. Mutable-object camp: ruby, cpp (std::string), rust (String grows in place; &str is the immutable view). Probe a is the one that matters -- two names on one string, edited through the first, read through the second -- and it is the shared-mutation question the census flags as the residue. Probe b is assignment into a position. Both live in their own programs because for several languages the answer is a REFUSAL at build time, which is itself the guarantee: rust's borrow checker forbids editing while a second name reads (probe a), and go, java, kotlin, dart and typescript have no position-assignment at all (probe b). php's expectation on probe b is the census's own placement of php in the immutable-value camp: the string does not change.",
